n21596.content:
\begin{definition}[Edge subdivision: replace $e = uv$ by a length-2 path through a new vertex]
\lean{SimpleGraph.subdivide}
\label{SimpleGraph.subdivide}
\leanok
Subdivision grows the vertex set by one, so unlike edge deletion it *changes the
carrier type*: \texttt{V $\oplus$ Unit}, with \texttt{Sum.inr ()} the new midpoint.  Adjacency:
\item \texttt{inl x \textasciitilde{} inl y} iff \texttt{G.Adj x y} and $s(x, y) \ne e$ --- the old edges, minus $e$;
\item \texttt{inl x \textasciitilde{} inr \_} iff $x \in e$               --- the two new half-edges;
\item \texttt{inr \_ \textasciitilde{} inr \_} never                     --- no loop at the midpoint.

Corollary 3.2.2 only, via the book's remark that *"the class of blocks with at
least three vertices is closed under the operation of subdivision"*.  That
closure property is itself unproved here, which is the main obstacle to
\texttt{block\_edges\_on\_common\_cycle}.
\end{definition}

n21597.content:
\begin{theorem}[edgeConnectivity\_le\_minDegree]
\lean{SimpleGraph.edgeConnectivity_le_minDegree}
\label{SimpleGraph.edgeConnectivity_le_minDegree}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsEdgeCut, SimpleGraph.edgeConnectivity}
, second inequality: $\kappa' \le \delta$.

If $G$ is trivial then $\kappa' = 0 \le \delta$.  Otherwise take $v$ with $d(v) = \delta$; the
$\delta$ edges incident with $v$ form an edge cut, since deleting them isolates $v$.
Hence $\kappa' \le d(v) = \delta$.
\end{theorem}

n21598.content:
\begin{theorem}[Helper for Theorem 3.1: $\kappa \le \nu - 1$ always]
\lean{SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity_le_card_pred}
\label{SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity_le_card_pred}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsVertexCut, SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity}
A vertex cut is by definition a \emph{proper} subset of $V$, so it omits some vertex
and therefore has at most $\nu - 1$ elements; and in the no-vertex-cut case $\kappa$ is
\emph{defined} to be $\nu - 1$.  Either way $\kappa \le \nu - 1$.
\end{theorem}

n21599.content:
\begin{theorem}[Core of the block criterion: a connected graph on at least three vertices with no cut vertex has $\kappa\dots$]
\lean{SimpleGraph.two_le_vertexConnectivity_of_no_cutVertex}
\label{SimpleGraph.two_le_vertexConnectivity_of_no_cutVertex}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsCutVertex, SimpleGraph.IsVertexCut, SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity}
A minimum vertex cut cannot have size $0$ --- that would make $G$ disconnected,
contradicting \texttt{hconn} --- nor size $1$, since that would exhibit a cut vertex,
contradicting \texttt{hnocut}.  And if no vertex cut exists at all then
$\kappa = \nu - 1 \ge 3 - 1 = 2$.
\end{theorem}

n21601.content:
\begin{theorem}[Successor step, part 1: from a minimum edge cut of size $k + 1$, deleting one of its edges drops the edge\dots]
\lean{SimpleGraph.exists_deleteEdge_edgeConnectivity_eq}
\label{SimpleGraph.exists_deleteEdge_edgeConnectivity_eq}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsEdgeCut, SimpleGraph.edgeConnectivity}
Indices are shifted by one to stay in $\mathbb{N}$: the book's $\kappa'(G) = k$ with
$\kappa'(H) = k - 1$ is stated here as $\kappa'(G) = k + 1$ with $\kappa'(G - e) = k$, which
avoids truncated subtraction entirely.

Take $F$ realising $\kappa'(G) = k + 1$; it is nonempty, so pick $e \in F$.  Then
$F \ {e}$ is an edge cut of $G - e$ of size $k$, giving $\kappa'(G - e) \le k$; and
$\kappa'(G - e) \ge k$, because a smaller cut of $G - e$ together with $e$ would be a
cut of $G$ of size $< k + 1$, contradicting minimality.
\end{theorem}

n21602.content:
\begin{theorem}[Successor step, part 2: deleting a single edge drops $\kappa$ by at most one]
\lean{SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity_le_deleteEdge_succ}
\label{SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity_le_deleteEdge_succ}
\uses{SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity}
This is the heart of the induction, isolated as a standalone inequality
$\kappa(G) \le \kappa(G - e) + 1$ so that \texttt{vertexConnectivity\_le\_edgeConnectivity} becomes a
three-line \texttt{calc}.  Writing $H = G - e$, the book's case analysis is:
\item $H$ has a complete spanning subgraph $\Rightarrow$ so does $G$, and $\kappa(G) = \kappa(H)$;
\item otherwise take $S$ a vertex cut of $H$ with $|S| = \kappa(H)$, and split:
\item $G - S$ already disconnected $\Rightarrow$ $S$ is a vertex cut of $G$, so $\kappa(G) \le |S|$;
\item $G - S$ connected $\Rightarrow$ $e$ is a cut edge (bridge) of $G - S$, and then
\item $\nu(G - S) = 2$ $\Rightarrow$ $\kappa(G) \le \nu(G) - 1 = |S| + 1$, by
      \texttt{vertexConnectivity\_le\_card\_pred};
\item $\nu(G - S) \ge 3$ $\Rightarrow$ by Exercise 2.3.1(a) $G - S$ has a cut vertex $w$, and
      $S \cup {w}$ is a vertex cut of $G$, so $\kappa(G) \le |S| + 1$.

Every branch lands at $\kappa(G) \le \kappa(H) + 1$, which is the statement.
\end{theorem}

n21603.content:
\begin{theorem}[exists\_isVertexCut\_singleton\_of\_isBridge]
\lean{SimpleGraph.exists_isVertexCut_singleton_of_isBridge}
\label{SimpleGraph.exists_isVertexCut_singleton_of_isBridge}
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsVertexCut}
, used by \texttt{vertexConnectivity\_le\_deleteEdge\_succ}.

Only part (a) is needed.  Since $G$ is connected, $\omega(G) = 1$, so the conclusion
$\omega(G - v) > \omega(G)$ is "$G - v$ is disconnected" --- i.e. \texttt{$\exists$ w, G.IsVertexCut \{w\}}.

The book leaves this as an exercise, so there is no book proof to quote; the
argument below is the standard one.
\end{theorem}

n21604.content:
\begin{theorem}[vertexConnectivity\_le\_edgeConnectivity]
\lean{SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity_le_edgeConnectivity}
\label{SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity_le_edgeConnectivity}
\uses{SimpleGraph.edgeConnectivity, SimpleGraph.exists_deleteEdge_edgeConnectivity_eq, SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity, SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity_eq_zero_of_edgeConnectivity_eq_zero, SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity_le_deleteEdge_succ}
, first inequality: $\kappa \le \kappa'$.

Induction on $\kappa'$.  Base $\kappa' = 0$: $G$ is trivial or disconnected, so $\kappa = 0$.
Step: given $\kappa'(G) = k + 1$, pick $e$ in a minimum edge cut; then
$\kappa'(G - e) = k$, so $\kappa(G - e) \le k$ by the induction hypothesis, and
$\kappa(G) \le \kappa(G - e) + 1 \le k + 1 = \kappa'(G)$.

The entire four-way case analysis of the book's second paragraph has been
factored out into \texttt{vertexConnectivity\_le\_deleteEdge\_succ}, so what remains here
is exactly the induction skeleton.
\end{theorem}

