theorem 11.5 the minimum number of edges separating a \emph{specific} pair equals the
maximum number of edge-disjoint paths between them.  Minimising over pairs converts
the global statement into the local one.  This is the edge form of Menger's theorem
quoted without proof in \S{}3.2, generalising exercise 3.2.1 ($k = 2$) to all $k$.

Uses the local \texttt{edgeConnectivity} copy.  \textbf{!} Watch the \texttt{sInf $\emptyset$ = 0}
boundary flagged there: on a one-vertex graph no edge cut exists, so
\texttt{edgeConnectivity = 0}, while the right-hand side is vacuously true for every $k$
(there is no pair $u \ne v$).  So the $\leftrightarrow$ fails at $k \ge 1$, \texttt{card V $\le$ 1} --- a
\texttt{[Nontrivial V]} hypothesis is the fix, exactly as in chapter 10's
\texttt{associatedDigraph\_isKArcConnected\_iff}.
\end{theorem}

n21997.content:
\begin{theorem}[menger\_vertex\_digraph]
\lean{Networks.menger_vertex_digraph}
\label{Networks.menger_vertex_digraph}
\uses{Networks.maxInternallyDisjointDirectedPaths, Networks.minVerticesDestroyingDirectedPaths}
\textbf{!} The hypothesis \texttt{$\lnot$ D.Adj x y} is load-bearing: an arc $(x, y)$ cannot
be destroyed by deleting \emph{intermediate} vertices, so without it the right-hand side
would be unattainable while the left is at least $1$.

\textbf{!} Step 1 must contend with \texttt{splitDigraph} splitting $x$ and $y$
as well, contrary to the book --- see that definition's docstring.  Concretely, a
$D'$-path from \texttt{inr x} to \texttt{inl y} never traverses $x$'s or $y$'s corridor, so the
correspondence is unharmed; but that fact needs stating rather than assuming.
\end{theorem}

n21998.content:
\begin{theorem}[menger\_vertex\_graph]
\lean{Networks.menger_vertex_graph}
\label{Networks.menger_vertex_graph}
\uses{Networks.maxInternallyDisjointPaths, Networks.minVerticesDestroyingPaths}
The most quoted form of Menger's theorem, and the one \S{}3.2 announced
without proof.  The number of independent routes between two stations equals the
number of intermediate stations an adversary must destroy to sever them --- the vertex
analogue of max-flow min-cut duality, specialising at $k = 2$ to Whitney's theorem
3.2.

Note step 2 is genuinely easier than the corresponding step of
theorem 11.5, so this is the better of the two undirected Menger theorems to attempt
first.
\end{theorem}

n21999.content:
\begin{theorem}[k\_connected\_iff\_internally\_disjoint\_paths]
\lean{Networks.k_connected_iff_internally_disjoint_paths}
\label{Networks.k_connected_iff_internally_disjoint_paths}
\uses{Networks.maxInternallyDisjointPaths, Networks.vertexConnectivity}
$k$-connectivity says no $k - 1$ vertices disconnect the graph; theorem
11.7 converts the local separation number for a nonadjacent pair into a path count,
and minimising over pairs converts global into local.

\textbf{!} B\&M call this \emph{immediate}; it is not --- step 2 is genuine work,
and the $k = 2$ case alone is a full proof in the repo (\texttt{TwoConnected.lean:537}).
Uses the local \texttt{vertexConnectivity}, whose $\nu - 1$ fallback for complete graphs
interacts with step 2 (a complete graph has \emph{only} adjacent pairs), so check that
boundary.  \texttt{h : k + 1 $\le$ card V} is the book's $\nu \ge k + 1$.
\end{theorem}

n22014.content:
\begin{definition}[$a$ and $b$ are consecutive vertices of attachment of the bridge of $e$: both are attachments, and no\dots]
\lean{SimpleGraph.ConsecutiveAttach}
\label{SimpleGraph.ConsecutiveAttach}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.Walk.OnArc, SimpleGraph.attach}
The arc from $a$ forwards to $b$ is then exactly one \textbf{segment} of the
bridge --- an arc of $C$ with attachments at both ends and none in between.  Ranging
over all consecutive pairs recovers the book's partition of $C$.

"No attachment strictly between" is phrased as: every attachment
on the closed arc is one of the two endpoints.  This is what makes the segments
partition $C$ rather than merely cover it.  The definition does not require the
bridge to have $k \ge 2$ attachments; with fewer, no pair satisfies it, and \texttt{Avoids}
below is then false for want of a witness --- which is the right answer, since a
$0$- or $1$-bridge has no segments to contain anything.
\end{definition}

n22015.content:
\begin{definition}[Two bridges of a cycle avoid one another: all the attachments of one lie in a single segment of the other]
\lean{SimpleGraph.Avoids}
\label{SimpleGraph.Avoids}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.ConsecutiveAttach, SimpleGraph.Walk.OnArc, SimpleGraph.attach}
Cut the cycle at one bridge's attachment points, producing arcs; the
two bridges avoid each other when the second's attachments all fall inside a single
arc, so both can be drawn on the same side of $C$ without crossing.

The disjunction mirrors the book's "of one bridge \dots of the
other", which is not symmetric on its face --- the definition says only that \emph{some}
assignment of the two roles works.  A segment of the bridge of $e$ is the arc
\texttt{OnArc a b} for $a$, $b$ consecutive attachments of $e$.
\end{definition}

n22023.content:
\begin{theorem}[bridgeOf\_connected]
\lean{SimpleGraph.bridgeOf_connected}
\label{SimpleGraph.bridgeOf_connected}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.bridgeOf}
A bridge is one connected lump: its edges all lie in one $\sim$-class,
and $\sim$ relates edges precisely when a walk avoiding $H$'s interior joins them.

Together with \texttt{exists\_path\_internallyDisjoint} this is one of
two facts B\&M wave through and that everything downstream (theorems 9.6, 9.7 and
exercise 9.4.3) actually consumes.  Step 3 is the real content and is worth
extracting as a lemma about \texttt{bridgeRel} chains, since the same induction is needed
for the companion result.
\end{theorem}

n22027.content:
\begin{theorem}[exists\_bridge\_off\_longest\_cycle]
\lean{SimpleGraph.exists_bridge_off_longest_cycle}
\label{SimpleGraph.exists_bridge_off_longest_cycle}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.Walk.IsLongestCycle, SimpleGraph.bridgeOf}
Since $G$ is not hamiltonian the longest cycle misses some vertex;
connectivity joins that vertex to the cycle, and the joining path's edges lie in a
bridge that therefore reaches off $C$.  This is the setup for the whole exercise ---
that bridge is the object whose attachments part (ii) constrains, and Chv\'atal--Erd\H{o}s
falls out of those constraints.

Note this is the \emph{only} one of the three 9.4.3 statements that
carries \texttt{hnh}; part (ii) as stated below drops it, which is where its defect comes
from.
\end{theorem}

n22028.content:
\begin{theorem}[succ\_attachments\_not\_adj]
\lean{SimpleGraph.succ_attachments_not_adj}
\label{SimpleGraph.succ_attachments_not_adj}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.Walk.IsLongestCycle, SimpleGraph.attach, SimpleGraph.bridgeOf}
The successors of a bridge's attachment points form an *independent
set*.  That is exactly the leverage Chv\'atal--Erd\H{o}s needs: a bridge with many
attachments yields a large independent set, which $\alpha \le \kappa$ forbids.

Indices are into the walk (\texttt{c.getVert i}), so $i + 1$ is the
successor along $c$; the wraparound at the end of the support is handled by
\texttt{getVert} saturating, which is why step 3 must be phrased as two explicit arcs
rather than as index arithmetic mod $n$.
\end{theorem}

n22035.content:
\begin{theorem}[tree\_unique\_path]
\lean{SimpleGraph.tree_unique_path}
\label{SimpleGraph.tree_unique_path}
\leanok
A tree has exactly enough edges to hold the vertices together and not one
more: connectivity gives at least one route between any two vertices, acyclicity
forbids a second, since two distinct routes always enclose a cycle.  \textbf{!} The converse
holds for loopless graphs (exercise 2.1.1), so "unique paths between all pairs" is an
equivalent definition of a tree --- that direction is not stated here.
\end{theorem}

n22036.content:
\begin{theorem}[tree\_card\_edgeFinset]
\lean{SimpleGraph.tree_card_edgeFinset}
\label{SimpleGraph.tree_card_edgeFinset}
\leanok
$\nu - 1$ edges is exactly the price of connecting $\nu$ vertices acyclically
--- corollary 2.4.2 shows it is also the minimum price of connecting them at all.

Stated additively as $\varepsilon + 1 = \nu$ to avoid truncated
$\mathbb{N}$-subtraction.
\end{theorem}

n22037.content:
\begin{theorem}[tree\_two\_leaves]
\lean{SimpleGraph.tree_two_leaves}
\label{SimpleGraph.tree_two_leaves}
\leanok
A degree-one vertex of a tree is a \textbf{leaf}.  \textbf{!} B\&M offer a more
illuminating alternative (exercise 2.1.2): take a longest path; both endpoints have
degree one, since an extra neighbour would either extend the path or close a cycle.
That route would be shorter in Lean too, if \texttt{exists\_path\_length\_of\_minDegree}-style
machinery from chapter 1 were available.
\end{theorem}

n22039.content:
\begin{theorem}[connected\_isTree\_iff\_forall\_edge\_isBridge]
\lean{SimpleGraph.connected_isTree_iff_forall_edge_isBridge}
\label{SimpleGraph.connected_isTree_iff_forall_edge_isBridge}
\leanok
A tree is exactly a connected graph with no redundancy --- every single
edge indispensable for keeping it in one piece.  \textbf{!} Exercise 2.2.1, not stated here,
drops the connectivity hypothesis: $G$ is a \textbf{forest} iff every edge is a cut
edge.
\end{theorem}

n22040.content:
\begin{theorem}[exists\_spanningTree]
\lean{SimpleGraph.exists_spanningTree}
\label{SimpleGraph.exists_spanningTree}
\leanok
Keep deleting redundant edges --- those lying on cycles --- until none remain;
what is left still connects everything but has no cycle.  \textbf{*} This is the workhorse of
the rest of the chapter: corollary 2.4.2, theorem 2.6 and corollary 2.7 all open by
invoking it.
\end{theorem}

n22041.content:
\begin{theorem}[connected\_card\_edgeFinset\_ge]
\lean{SimpleGraph.connected_card_edgeFinset_ge}
\label{SimpleGraph.connected_card_edgeFinset_ge}
\leanok
$\nu - 1$ edges is the minimum price of connecting $\nu$ vertices, and trees
are precisely the connected graphs paying exactly that price.  \textbf{!} Compare exercise
2.1.5, not stated here: for a graph with exactly $\nu - 1$ edges, connected, acyclic and
tree are all equivalent.
\end{theorem}

n22043.content:
\begin{theorem}[cotree\_bond]
\lean{SimpleGraph.cotree_bond}
\label{SimpleGraph.cotree_bond}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsBond}
The exact mirror of theorem 2.5, with \emph{cycle} $\to$ \emph{bond} and *spanning
tree\emph{ $\to$ }cotree*.  \textbf{*} Chapter 12's \texttt{fundamentalBondVertexSet} is exactly the $S$ of
step 3, and cites this theorem by name.

\textbf{!} Both parts consume \texttt{IsBond}, whose $\emptyset$ defect is active
here --- but \texttt{hG : G.Connected} is available as a hypothesis, which is precisely the
condition under which the defect disappears.  Use it explicitly rather than relying on
it silently.
\end{theorem}

n22044.content:
\begin{theorem}[tree\_isCutVertex\_iff\_degree]
\lean{SimpleGraph.tree_isCutVertex_iff_degree}
\label{SimpleGraph.tree_isCutVertex_iff_degree}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsCutVertex}
In a tree the cut vertices are exactly the internal (non-leaf) vertices.

\textbf{!} Step 2's exercise 2.1.5 is the one real import; Mathlib's
\texttt{IsAcyclic} API may supply it more directly than reproving the book's route.
\end{theorem}

n22045.content:
\begin{theorem}[connected\_two\_non\_cutVertices]
\lean{SimpleGraph.connected_two_non_cutVertices}
\label{SimpleGraph.connected_two_non_cutVertices}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsCutVertex}
However tangled a connected graph is, there are always at least two
vertices you can delete without breaking it apart.
\end{theorem}

n22046.content:
\begin{theorem}[numSpanningTrees\_deletion\_contraction]
\lean{SimpleGraph.numSpanningTrees_deletion_contraction}
\label{SimpleGraph.numSpanningTrees_deletion_contraction}
\uses{SimpleGraph.contract, SimpleGraph.numSpanningTrees}
Iterating the recursion reduces any graph to trivial ones and so computes
$\tau$ --- though B\&M note it is impractical for large graphs, which is what motivates
chapter 12's determinant formula.

\textbf{\textbf{!} Currently vacuous.}  \texttt{contract} has a \texttt{sorry} body, so $G \cdot e$ is opaque and this
statement relates opaque quantities.  Worse, \texttt{contract} is a \textbf{same-carrier}
placeholder, so even an honest body on $V$ could not satisfy $\nu(G\cdot e) = \nu - 1$ and step
3's bijection would be false.  Repair \texttt{contract} on the quotient carrier first --- see its
docstring.
\end{theorem}

n22047.content:
\begin{theorem}[cayley]
\lean{SimpleGraph.cayley}
\label{SimpleGraph.cayley}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.numSpanningTrees}
\textbf{!} B\&M caution that $n^{n-2}$ counts \emph{distinct} spanning trees, not
non-isomorphic ones: $K_{6}$ has six non-isomorphic ones but $6^{4} = 1296$ distinct.

Mathlib has no Cayley formula and no Pr\"ufer correspondence, so all
four steps are from scratch --- the largest single item in the chapter.
\end{theorem}

n22050.content:
\begin{definition}[$G$ is $k$-critical: $k$-chromatic and critical]
\lean{SimpleGraph.IsKCritical}
\label{SimpleGraph.IsKCritical}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsCritical}
$k$-critical graphs are the irreducible witnesses to needing $k$
colours.  Exercise 8.1.7 classifies the small cases completely --- the only
1-critical graph is $K_{1}$, the only 2-critical graph is $K_{2}$, and the 3-critical
graphs are exactly the odd cycles --- and Brooks' theorem 8.4 consumes precisely
that classification to get its standing hypothesis $k \ge 4$.

\texttt{G.chromaticNumber = k} compares an $\mathbb{N}\infty$ with a coerced $\mathbb{N}$,
so $k$-chromaticity here also carries the information that $\chi(G) \ne \top$.
\end{definition}

n22063.content:
\begin{theorem}[exists\_isKCritical\_subgraph]
\lean{SimpleGraph.exists_isKCritical_subgraph}
\label{SimpleGraph.exists_isKCritical_subgraph}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsKCritical}
Start with a $k$-chromatic graph and keep deleting vertices and edges
as long as the chromatic number stays at $k$.  The graph is finite, so this stops;
what remains is still $k$-chromatic but every proper subgraph of it needs fewer
colours --- that is, it is $k$-critical.  This is the reduction that makes critical
graphs worth studying at all: any statement proved for $k$-critical graphs
transfers to all $k$-chromatic ones.  Corollary 8.1.1, Brooks' theorem 8.4 and
theorem 8.5 all open by invoking it.

Only \texttt{[Finite V]} is needed, not \texttt{[Fintype V]}: the argument
uses finiteness solely to well-found the minimisation in step 2.
\end{theorem}

n22080.content:
\begin{theorem}[isKCritical\_two\_iff]
\lean{SimpleGraph.isKCritical_two_iff}
\label{SimpleGraph.isKCritical_two_iff}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsKCritical}
A 2-chromatic graph has at least one edge, and a single edge already
needs both colours; criticality leaves no room for anything else.

\texttt{$\top$ : SimpleGraph (Fin 2)} is $K_{2}$.  Step 3 is the shared
"a subgraph attaining $\chi(G)$ must be $\top$" lemma noted under the 3-critical case.
\end{theorem}

n22082.content:
\begin{theorem}[neighborSet\_not\_subset]
\lean{SimpleGraph.IsCritical.neighborSet_not_subset}
\label{SimpleGraph.IsCritical.neighborSet_not_subset}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsCritical}
In a critical graph no vertex is redundant, and a vertex whose
neighbourhood is contained in another's is exactly redundant: it can simply
inherit that vertex's colour.  Note the argument needs $u$ and $v$ \emph{nonadjacent} to
work, and gets it for free --- if $u ~ v$ then $v \in N(u) \subseteq N(v)$, contradicting
looplessness.

Stated with $\lnot (\dots \subseteq \dots)$, matching the book's $\not\subset$.  \texttt{[Nonempty V]}
is needed to know $\chi(G) \ge 1$ in step 1.
\end{theorem}

n22083.content:
\begin{theorem}[no\_isKCritical\_card\_eq\_succ]
\lean{SimpleGraph.no_isKCritical_card_eq_succ}
\label{SimpleGraph.no_isKCritical_card_eq_succ}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsKCritical}
So $k$-critical graphs come in sizes $k$ (the complete graph $K_k$)
and $k + 2$ or more, never $k + 1$.  The reason is that one vertex of slack forces
the complement to be a matching, and a matched pair has \emph{identical}
neighbourhoods --- the most extreme violation of part (a).  Exercise 8.1.12 asks for
4-critical graphs on $n$ vertices for $n = 4$ and all $n \ge 6$; the gap at $n = 5$
is exactly this result.

Step 2's degree identity \texttt{G.degree v + G$^{c}$.degree v = card V - 1}
is the workhorse and is worth stating as its own \texttt{have}.  Take care that $k - 1$ in
step 1 is $\mathbb{N}$-subtraction --- the case $k = 0$ should be dispatched separately (a
0-chromatic graph has an empty carrier, so \texttt{card V = 1} is impossible).
\end{theorem}

n22091.content:
\begin{theorem}[brooks\_iff\_edge\_bound]
\lean{SimpleGraph.brooks_iff_edge_bound}
\label{SimpleGraph.brooks_iff_edge_bound}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.IsKCritical}
Theorem 8.1 already gives $2\varepsilon \ge \nu(k-1)$ for a $k$-critical graph; the
exercise's content is the extra $+1$, which says the bound is never \emph{exactly}
attained --- not every vertex can have degree exactly $k - 1$.  That is precisely
Brooks in disguise: a $(k-1)$-regular critical graph would have $\chi = k = \Delta + 1$,
which Brooks forbids outside the two exceptional families.

Both sides are pinned to \texttt{Type} (not \texttt{Type*}) so that the two
quantifications range over the same universe and the $\leftrightarrow$ is well-formed.  Note the
statement quantifies over graphs, so each direction \emph{instantiates} the assumed side
at a graph it constructs --- this is an equivalence of schemas, not of propositions
about one fixed $G$.
\end{theorem}

n22095.content:
\begin{theorem}[fourChromatic\_hasK4Subdivision]
\lean{SimpleGraph.fourChromatic_hasK4Subdivision}
\label{SimpleGraph.fourChromatic_hasK4Subdivision}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.HasK4Subdivision}
This is the case $k = 4$ of Haj\'os' conjecture, settled by Dirac
(1952).  The book notes the conjecture in general is *known to be a very difficult
problem*, and mentions Hadwiger's related conjecture, whose case $k = 5$ Wagner
showed equivalent to the four-colour conjecture of chapter 9.

Step 1's monotonicity lemma is what licenses the book's
"without loss of generality" and should be built first --- without it the reduction
to the critical subgraph is not available.  The induction in step 2 is on the
carrier's cardinality, but cases A and B produce graphs on \emph{different carriers}
(subgraph coercions), so the induction is cleanest stated over all graphs on all
carriers of bounded size rather than over subgraphs of a fixed $G$.
\end{theorem}

n22096.content:
\begin{theorem}[hasK4Subdivision\_of\_few\_low\_degree]
\lean{SimpleGraph.hasK4Subdivision_of_few_low_degree}
\label{SimpleGraph.hasK4Subdivision_of_few_low_degree}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.HasK4Subdivision}
Essentially-all vertices having degree at least three is enough to
force a topological $K_{4}$, with no colouring hypothesis at all.  So this is a purely
degree-theoretic sufficient condition where theorem 8.5 gives a chromatic one.
Neither is necessary: a subdivision of $K_{4}$ can occur in graphs that are only
3-chromatic --- the book's own example, a 4-cycle being a subdivision of $K_{3}$, makes
the analogous point one dimension down.

"At most one vertex of degree less than three" is a \texttt{Finset}
cardinality bound on \texttt{univ.filter (fun v => G.degree v < 3)}.  This is the
statement exercise 8.3.2(a) reduces to, so filling that one first is not an
option --- the dependency runs this way.
\end{theorem}

n22102.content:
\begin{theorem}[numColorings\_le\_of\_connected]
\lean{SimpleGraph.numColorings_le_of_connected}
\label{SimpleGraph.numColorings_le_of_connected}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.numColorings}
Among connected graphs on $\nu$ vertices, trees are exactly the ones
with the most colourings: every extra edge kills at least one colouring of the
spanning tree.

The two clauses are bundled as a conjunction because the book
states them together; only the second needs the guard on $k$.  Note step 5 is where
the whole content sits --- step 2's inequality is nearly formal.
\end{theorem}

n22105.content:
\begin{theorem}[numColorings\_wheel]
\lean{SimpleGraph.numColorings_wheel}
\label{SimpleGraph.numColorings_wheel}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.numColorings, SimpleGraph.wheel}
A wheel with $n$ spokes is $C_n \lor K_{1}$ --- a rim cycle plus a hub joined
to every rim vertex --- so its colourings are a hub colour together with a colouring
of the rim in the remaining palette.  Sanity check: at $k = 3$ and odd $n$ the
formula gives $3\cdot 1 + (-1)\cdot 3\cdot 1 = 0$, matching that a wheel with an odd rim is
4-chromatic and so has no 3-colouring at all.

$hn : 3 \le n$ is inherited from exercise 8.4.4 (the rim must be a
genuine cycle); $hk : 1 \le k$ is what step 4 consumes.
\end{theorem}

n22107.content:
\begin{theorem}[numColorings\_union\_mul\_inter]
\lean{SimpleGraph.numColorings_union_mul_inter}
\label{SimpleGraph.numColorings_union_mul_inter}
\leanok
\uses{SimpleGraph.numColorings}
A gluing formula.  A colouring of the union is a pair of colourings of
the parts agreeing on the overlap; because the overlap is \emph{complete}, its vertices
must take distinct colours, which makes the two sides' colourings matchable in a
uniform number of ways.  Together with exercise 8.4.6 (components multiply) this is
one of the practical tools for computing chromatic polynomials by decomposition.

Phrased on the \texttt{Subgraph} lattice --- $\sqcup$ and $\sqcap$ of \texttt{G.Subgraph}
--- because \texttt{SimpleGraph V} has no notion of union/intersection with differing vertex
sets.  \texttt{hcomp : (G' $\sqcap$ H').coe = $\top$} says the overlap is complete *as a graph on its
own vertex set*.  Note that the book's identity is stated multiplicatively precisely
to avoid the division that step 5 also avoids.
\end{theorem}

n22111.content:
\begin{theorem}[descartes\_construction]
\lean{SimpleGraph.descartes_construction}
\label{SimpleGraph.descartes_construction}
\leanok
Mycielski's construction (theorem 8.7) removes triangles but still
leaves 5-cycles.  Descartes' construction is stronger --- it keeps the girth at six
or more, so the graphs have no short cycles at all, and yet the chromatic number
still climbs without bound.  Blanche Descartes (1954) gave the original recursive
construction; the book calls Mycielski's the \emph{easier} one, which is why \S{}8.5 proves
theorem 8.7 in the text and relegates this to a starred exercise.

The conclusion is $\ge k + 1$, not $= k + 1$: the construction
gives a lower bound and the exercise asks only for that.  Likewise \texttt{6 $\le$ girth}
rather than $= 6$.  Step 1 is most of the Lean work --- the indexed family of copies
and the per-copy matching bijection have to be built explicitly, and \texttt{Fintype}
instances derived for the resulting carrier.
\end{theorem}

